_____ literally means “a throwing beside” — that is, a comparison or juxtaposition. Via Latin it made its way into English several separate times, with meanings that diverged so wildly that few speakers ever realize these words share a root. The first borrowing X came in the 13th century via Old French. Early Christian writers had used the Greek word to translate Hebrew mAshAl — the kind of teaching-by-comparison story that Jesus famously told. X now means a short narrative that illustrates a moral lesson. The second borrowing Y came in the 1570s, directly from Latin, into the technical vocabulary of mathematics. The Greek geometer Apollonius of Perga had used the same Greek word for one of the conic sections, which was borrowed as-is as Y. The third borrowing came in the early 1600s via French, where the meaning had narrowed from “narrative speech” to “word of honor.” A captured soldier could be released on his word of honor not to fight again. This gives us Z, the term still used today for the conditional release of prisoners. ID X (5 points), Y (5 points), and Z (5 points).", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T14:46:08Z", "username": "Nikhil Borra", "text": "Parable", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T14:46:10Z", "username": "Yash Agrawal", "text": "parabola", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T14:46:12Z", "username": "Abhishek Saluja", "text": "Moral", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T14:46:13Z", "username": "Nikhil Borra", "text": "Parole", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T14:46:14Z", "username": "Srijan Naayak", "text": "Parole", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T14:46:14Z", "